.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-stage-clean 1  "tapectl-stage-clean " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-stage\-clean \- Release staged slices from disk
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl stage clean\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-unit\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Release staged slices from disk
.PP
A stage set is released once it has a completed write and its unit is not below its copy floor; one below it is retained and named, and `\-\-force` releases it too. A set that spans cartridges is released slice by slice, each slice once it has its copies. A failed set is always swept.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Release unwritten sets and units below their copy floor too

Releases every staged set in scope, not just those with completed writes, and a unit\*(Aqs staged data that a bare `stage clean` would retain for being below its copy floor (the larger of its policy\*(Aqs min_copies and the sum of its copies_at targets, times the tape\-only multiplier for a tape\-only unit). It never releases a set a live write session or a pending `volume rewrite` is using.
.TP
\fB\-\-unit\fR \fI<UNIT>\fR
Only this unit\*(Aqs staged sets (repeat it for several)

Without `\-\-force`, the same copy\-floor split still applies within the named units: one below its own copy floor keeps its staged bytes (pass `\-\-force` to release it too, exactly as for the whole\-archive form). A failed stage set is always swept, whatever the scope. An unknown unit name is an error, not a silent no\-op.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR \fI<VERSION>\fR
Only this version of the one unit `\-\-unit` names

Requires exactly one `\-\-unit`; zero or more than one is a usage error naming the constraint, never a silent widening back to the whole unit. So a `\-\-force` release meant for one under\-written version of a unit can never also release a DIFFERENT, still\-unwritten version of that same unit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
